          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Early Life and Injury</strong><br>\nFrida Kahlo was born in 1907 in Coyoacán, Mexico City, Mexico. At the age of six, she contracted polio, which left her with a limp, a condition that isolated her from other children. In 1925, she was severely injured in a bus accident, leading to a long period of recovery that influenced her decision to begin painting.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Art as a Reflection of Life</strong><br>\nKahlo's paintings are deeply personal, often depicting her own pain and suffering in a direct and stark manner. Her work is characterized by its honesty and striking imagery, with self-portraits making up a significant portion of her oeuvre.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Marriage to Diego Rivera</strong><br>\nKahlo married the muralist Diego Rivera in 1929. Their tumultuous relationship was fraught with mutual infidelities and professional tensions, yet they shared a deep love for each other and a passion for art and politics.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          }
        ],
