          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>The Blue House and Personal Space</strong><br>\nKahlo spent much of her life in her family home, known as the Blue House or Casa Azul, which became a meeting place for artists and intellectuals. After her death, it was transformed into a museum dedicated to her life and work.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Legacy and Influence</strong><br>\nKahlo's legacy extends beyond her art; she is celebrated as an icon of individuality and female creativity. She is also recognized for her influence on feminist and LGBTQ+ movements, as she explored themes of identity, post-colonialism, gender, class, and race in her work.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Death and Posthumous Fame</strong><br>\nKahlo died in 1954, her death was as dramatic as her life; she left behind a body of work that continues to resonate with audiences for its raw and gripping portrayal of the human condition. Her fame grew posthumously, and she is now regarded as one of the most significant artists of the 20th century.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3d655444-fd55-42f7-aba2-16a16db7dc58"
